# `arvo-standards`: the conventions as aliases, and what refuses to become one

**Canon this design declares.**
`ruling::the_format_spine_is_canon`, for arithmetic as an exact operation composed with a named
adaptation, and for the concept being closed while the inventory is open.
`ruling::behaviour_is_stated_per_declared_signature_and_the_premise_dissolves`, and through it
`proposal::every_operation_arvo_declares_is_a_function_of_the_declared_width`, which is the row this
crate answers and whose argument is the reason it exists.
`ruling::the_standards_bound_starts_at_two_and_reserves_the_rest`, for which conventions are in scope.
`ruling::the_standard_is_parity_in_output_not_in_the_internals`, for what being in scope demands.
`ruling::the_ambiguous_rounding_word_is_retired_for_six_explicit_names`, for the vocabulary a
convention's rounding method has to land in.
`ruling::never_a_runtime_check_and_one_lowered_path`, for what happens where it cannot.

## What this crate is

One alias per industrial convention, over the declared signatures `arvo-format` already carries, plus
the arms that check each alias against what its standard documents.

It ships no arithmetic. A convention here is a naming, and the test of whether the naming is honest is
that there is nothing behind it: the alias resolves to a `Signature` and every operation is the one
`arvo-format` already declared. A crate that had to compute something to make a convention work would
have found that the convention is not expressible, which is a finding rather than a feature.

## Why an alias is the whole obligation and not a shortcut to it

The spine says the concept is closed and the inventory of admitted instances is open. An instance
joins by supplying the concept's obligations, so a convention joins the same way anything else does,
and writing one as an alias is the admission rule being exercised rather than worked around.

That is also what makes the crate a test rather than a wrapper. The proposal this design answers puts
it directly:

> A convention that cannot be written as an alias over the primitives is a gap in the primitives, so
> the two standards refute a candidate rather than merely preferring one

So the interesting output of this crate is its refusals. Every place a convention names something the
floor cannot express is recorded below, and each is a claim about `arvo-format` rather than about the
convention.

## What a convention alias has to supply

Three coordinates and no fourth, because a declared signature carries three and a convention that
needed more would be describing a different concept.

**The representable set**, as a `Format`: the ambient domain the values are drawn from, the slot range
the width admits, the quantum law that fixes the step, and the phase. Every convention in scope has
phase zero, and the coordinate is still carried rather than assumed, because a convention with a bias
would land here and nowhere else.

**The adaptation**, as an `Adaptation`: the rounding mode and the overflow policy the convention's
math settings name. This is where a convention's per-operation settings live, and it is why they are
part of the signature rather than an argument: two settings over one format are two declared
signatures.

**Nothing about placement.** A convention says what the values are and how a result lands on them. Where
the bits sit is `arvo-placement`'s and no convention in scope has an opinion the design has to carry.

## MATLAB `fi` and `fimath`

The first member, and the one the standards bound names first.

A fi carries a signedness, a word length and a fraction length, and its real-world value is the stored
integer divided by two to the power of the fraction length. A fimath carries a rounding method and an
overflow action. Each of those five is one of arvo's coordinates written in MATLAB's words, and the
table reads left to right from the convention's name to the thing that already exists here.

| What MATLAB calls it | What carries it |
|---|---|
| signedness | `Signed` or `Unsigned`, which is the choice of slot range |
| word length | the width parameter of that slot range |
| fraction length | `FractionLength`, a quantum law whose exponent is its negation |
| rounding method | `Rounding` |
| overflow action | `Overflow` |

MATLAB's property names are left unbackticked on purpose. Backticks here mean an item that exists in
this source tree, and four of these are another system's vocabulary that never will be.

The negation is the one place the spelling fights. An exponent written as a negated parameter is an
expression in a const generic argument position, which needs a feature this workspace forbids. Naming
what the expression computes dissolves it: the fraction length is a quantum law of its own, its
exponent is that negation computed in an impl body where an arbitrary expression is legal, and the
alias bounds on the `Quantum` contract instead of on the expression. Nothing about what is constrained
changes.

A negative fraction length is ordinary in the convention and needs no separate case, since it is the
same negation landing on a positive exponent.

### The overflow map is total

`Wrap` and `Saturate` are the two overflow actions and both are shipped policies. Nothing is
marked here.

### The rounding map is not total, and this is a finding about arvo

Four of MATLAB's six rounding methods land on a shipped mode. Ceiling is `Ceil`, Floor is `Floor`,
Zero is `TowardZero`, and Convergent is `HalfEven`.

The two nearest-with-ties methods do not resolve. MathWorks documents Nearest as ties toward positive
infinity and Round as ties toward the greater absolute value, and those are two different operations.
arvo's vocabulary has one nearest-with-ties-not-to-even name, `half_up`, and what it means is open:
`question::is_the_rounding_vocabulary_complete_at_six` asks whether the vocabulary is short a name,
treating ties away from zero as a mode outside the six, and
`question::which_tie_direction_an_unqualified_nearest_names` records that `half_up` is itself two
operations under a reading nobody has settled.

**So both are marked and neither is asserted.** A parity arm claiming Round is `half_up` would close
an open question inside a design, and one claiming Nearest is would close it the other way. The alias
names both methods, each carries a `FIXME` pointing at the row that decides it, and the parity arms
cover the four that are settled.

The honest reading of what the convention shows is stronger than either mark. MATLAB needs two
nearest-with-ties operations and arvo names one, so whatever `half_up` turns out to mean, one of the
two methods has no name in the vocabulary. That is the adequacy test finding a gap, and it is what
this crate is for.

### The width map is not total either, and this one is larger

MathWorks documents the word length from 1 to 65535 unsigned and 2 to 65535 signed. `arvo-format`
implements its slot range at widths 1 to 62, bounded by the slot index's own carrier: the count of
slots is two to the power of the width and two to the sixty-third does not fit a signed 64-bit
integer.

So a `fi` at word length 64 has no format. Not a rare declaration and not a corner: it is the width a
consumer reaches for without thinking about it.

**The alias does not work around this and does not report it at run time.** The width parameter carries
the slot range's own bound, so a declaration past it fails to build, with the diagnostic the slot range
already supplies. That is what `ruling::never_a_runtime_check_and_one_lowered_path` asks for, and it
is also the honest shape: the convention is expressible exactly where the floor can express it, and
the compile error is the report.

Worth saying what this is not. Behaviour is stated per declared signature and no operation reads a
carrier, so the bound is not a case of the carrier leaking into what a value means. What is bounded is
the vocabulary of widths that can be declared at all, and the convention wants three orders of
magnitude more of it.

## What a parity arm asserts

The standard's own documented answer, against what arvo returns. The number in the assertion comes
from the convention's published behaviour, and arvo is the side that has to agree.

An arm that computes both sides through arvo asserts that arvo agrees with itself, which is the
failure this crate would be most likely to ship, because it looks exactly like a parity arm and always
passes. So the constants are the standard's, written out, and the derivation that produced each one is
in the arm beside it.

Parity is in output. Reaching the documented answer by a different route is fine, because the
convention documents a result and not an implementation.

## What is here, and what is reserved

MATLAB `fi` and `fimath` are written. IEEE 754 is in scope and not yet started: half of its rounding
surface sits behind the same two open rows, and its interchange formats want the magnitude-indexed
quantum family rather than the constant one, which is a different reading of the same floor.

SystemC `sc_fixed`, Vitis `ap_fixed` and Algorithmic C `ac_fixed` are reserved rather than excluded.
A candidate that cannot express one of those is neither refuted nor cleared by anything here.
